# reading 2 of berkeley-master.md by claude-opus-5-5
# Berkeley's argument: the objector says we can imagine unperceived things; the reply is that
# imagining them is only framing ideas while we ourselves perceive them all the while.
# I take the main conclusion (S4) to be that you cannot conceive the objects of thought
# existing without the mind. S4 itself says only that imagining does not show you can, so
# this is a stronger reading.
atom	imagine_unperceived	you can imagine trees in a park, or books in a closet, existing with nobody by to perceive them
atom	framing_ideas	such imagining is only framing in your mind ideas you call books and trees while omitting to frame the idea of anyone who may perceive them
atom	you_perceive	you yourself perceive or think of them all the while
atom	conceive_mind_indep	you can conceive it possible that the objects of your thought exist without the mind
atom	conceive_unconceived	you conceive the objects existing unconceived or unthought of
atom	contemplate_own	when we do our utmost to conceive the existence of external bodies, we are only contemplating our own ideas
